This is the simplest relationship, but beginners often ask: "If it is 1-to-1, why not just put everything in one table?"
That is the right question to ask. Let's break it down.
1. The Concept: One-to-One (1:1)
 * Rule: One Record in Table A belongs to Exactly One Record in Table B.
 * Analogy: A Person and their Aadhar Card.
   * One Person has only one Aadhar Card.
   * One Aadhar Card belongs to only one Person.
2. The Real Reason: Why use it? 💡
Why not just add aadharNumber and address columns inside the User table?
Reason 1: Performance (Lazy Loading)
Imagine your User table is used for Login. You need it fast.
If you stuff the User table with heavy data (Biometrics, Full Address, Scans), fetching a User takes longer.
 * Solution: Keep User light (Username, Password). Put the heavy stuff in AadharDetails. We only load the heavy stuff when specifically asked.
Reason 2: Security
Your User table is accessed frequently. Your Aadhar data is sensitive.
 * Solution: By separating them, you can apply stricter security rules to the Aadhar table.
Reason 3: Business Logic (Optional Data)
Every user must have a username. But maybe having an Aadhar card is optional?
 * Solution: It's cleaner to have a separate table that might be empty, rather than a main table full of NULL values.
3. The Mini-Project: "User & Aadhar"
Let's build this. We will save a User and their Aadhar details in one shot.
Step 1: The Parent (User)
The User is the main entity. It "owns" the relationship.
package org.example.demo1.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import lombok.Data;

@Entity
@Data
@Table(name = "app_user") // 'User' is a reserved keyword in SQL, so we rename the table
public class User {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    
    private String name;
    private String email;

    // 🔗 THE RELATIONSHIP
    // cascade = ALL: Save User -> Automatically saves Aadhar too.
    @OneToOne(cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    @JoinColumn(name = "fk_aadhar_id") // This creates a column 'fk_aadhar_id' in the User table
    private AadharCard aadharCard;
}

Step 2: The Child (AadharCard)
This is just a standard entity.
package org.example.demo1.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import lombok.Data;

@Entity
@Data
public class AadharCard {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    
    private Long aadharNumber;
    private String address;
}

Step 3: The Controller (How to Save)
This is where the magic of CascadeType.ALL happens.
Endpoint: POST /user
Body:
{
    "name": "Rahul",
    "email": "rahul@example.com",
    "aadharCard": {
        "aadharNumber": 9988776655,
        "address": "Mumbai, India"
    }
}

The Code:
@PostMapping("/user")
public ResponseEntity<User> createUser(@RequestBody User user) {
    // We only save the User. 
    // Because of Cascade, Spring automatically:
    // 1. Saves AadharCard
    // 2. Saves User
    // 3. Links them together
    userRepo.save(user); 
    return new ResponseEntity<>(user, HttpStatus.CREATED);
}

🧠 Important Check
In One-to-One, the Foreign Key (the linking column) usually sits inside the Parent table (User).
 * The User table will have a column fk_aadhar_id containing the ID of the card.
 * The AadharCard table remains clean.
